{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1540938-rite-of-gaia\u2019s-rebirth","id":1540938,"title":"Rite of Gaia\u2019s Rebirth?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-08-23T07:52:08-07:00","id":1540938,"title":"Rite of Gaia\u2019s Rebirth?","content":"Ok, I just reread it, and something popped out me. It seems that only the rite master can sacrifice gnosis and stamina to make the rite work. So if a rite master has Stamina 5 and Gnosis 10, and he decides to kill himself to make the rite work, the best he can do is reclaim 10 acres of land.<br /> <br /> Is there an errata that clarifies that EVERYONE who participates can sacrifice gnosis and stamina to make the rite work?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-23T08:56:43-07:00","id":1540941,"title":"","content":"There is not. General consensus is that the writers didn't know how much an acre is. A while back I wrote a homebrew version that fixes these issues, though:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Rite of Gaia's Rebirth</b><br /> <br /> <b>Level:</b> 5<br /> <br /> <b>Description:</b> This extremely powerful rite was recently created by, and is currently only known to, the Sept of the First Rage. Gaia's Rebirth allows the ritemaster to sacrifice her own Gnosis and, if necessary, her own life to help Gaia retake territory humans defiled. The form of such corruption is irrelevant--all will crumble before the Talon's wrath.<br /> <br /> Even this rite, as mighty as it is, cannot completely undo humanity's works. Buildings do not vanish, but concrete cracks as trees grow to full size in minutes, steel is covered in dripping moss, and vines climb over brick. But pollutants and garbage will rapidly decay as the rite progresses.<br /> <br /> Performing this rite is complex. It requires a precise sense of timing, and the ritemaster must be guided by nothing but instinct. If she begins the rite even one minute too early or too late, the rite will fail but the energies released may well destroy her. Gaia's Rebirth can only be be performed on the last night of the waning crescent moon, the night before the start of the Ragabash cycle. The climax of rite must occur between the rising of the moon and rising of the sun, and the rite must be performed at the site to be purified (meaning a Red Talon cannot perform the rite outside city limits and expect the city itself to be consumed).<br /> <br /> The rite requires a special moot, attended by no fewer than six Garou (the ritemaster plus one werewolf of each auspice). the moot begins with howls to any totem spirits in the area (a caern totem, if the rite is performed at a caern, the totems of any packs present, and the tribe totem of all Garou present) in addition to an elaborate howl to Gaia Herself. The ritemaster must walk or run in a circle around the center of the area to be cleansed, howling to Gaia to awaken the spirits of the land to reclaim it. She gives of herself - this can be physical, in which case she bleeds onto ground, or spiritual, in which case she simply howls and gives up part of her own spirit. In either case, if the rite is performed correctly, the plant life in the area quickly overtakes any human &quot;development&quot; and restores Gaia's true order.<br /> <br /> The energies that build as this rite progresses will alert minions of the Weaver and the Wyrm--as well as some humans capable of detecting them--of a Garou presence, though they won't necessarily understand what the Talons are doing. Regardless, they will surely investigate, and will likely attack. Other Garou may also try to stop the Talons if they realize what is going on.<br /> <br /> <b>System:</b> The player must first roll Perception + Primal-Urge (difficulty 9) to be sure of the correct timing for the rite. The Storyteller may want to make this roll in secret, so that the player does not know the results. If the roll succeeds, the character knows when to start the rite so that the end coincides with the moon and sun properly. If the roll fails, the character is unsure, and must wait a full month before attempting the rite again. If the roll is a botch, the character is sure of the timing, but has actually miscalculated. She will automatically fail at performing the rite. The character must lead the moot as described above. At the moot's climax, the player rolls Wits + Rituals (difficulty 9, or 7 if the character has Ancestors at 2 or higher.)<br /> <br /> If the roll fails, the rite fails, and the character receives three health levels of aggravated damage. These appear as teardrop-shaped wounds on her body, similar to the &quot;Tears of Gaia&quot; commonly suffered by Garou who attempt the Rite of Caern Building. If the roll is a botch, the character loses Gnosis or Stamina as described below, but the rite still fails.<br /> <br /> If the roll succeeds, however, the player must decide how much land to reclaim from human corruption. For each dot of Stamina or Gnosis the ritemaster is willing to expend, roughly one square mile of land becomes overtaken with plant life. Other participants in the rite can spend Stamina and permanent Gnosis as well, though at a cost of two dots per one square mile.<br /> <br /> Plastics (including microplastics) in the area quickly rot into mundane organic compounds, non-supernatural toxins degrade, and heavy metals are bound into compounds where they can do no harm. Sharp glass and metal that might injure wildlife smooths.<br /> <br /> Large structures remain, though, and will become the caves and rock formations of the reclaimed area. The rite also does not affect living beings; humans in the area may be trapped in buildings or find themselves in the tree tops of a new forest but they won't die--from the rite, anyway. The sudden destruction of human infrastructure will spell doom for most of the population without an immediate, well-coordinated disaster response. </div> </div> </div>It's significantly stronger than you're proposing, but this is a rite that burns out the Talon's best ritemasters and is written to be their equivalent of a nuke. If it's not capable of taking out a city there's no point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28383-citrakayah","name":"Citrakayah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28383_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-08-25T12:37:04-07:00","id":1540999,"title":"","content":"Perfection!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}